#X obj 682 11 data.set;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 561 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 A set container: stores only unique elements \, in the
order in which they were first added. Adding a value that is already
in the set has no effect \, and removing a value that is not in the
set is also a no-op. Sets are useful when the presence or absence of
a value matters more than its position or count.;
#X text 240 184 Syntax: values are added one at a time (float or symbol)
\, as a list \, or with the add method. The set can be output as a
whole with a bang \, or converted to a list with [set->list].;
#X text 240 254 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 279 • Deduplication of a stream — removing repeated values
from an incoming stream \, so that each value is processed only once.;
#X text 250 334 • Tracking active events — keeping a set of currently
pressed keys \, active voices or live notes \, and checking membership
in a single operation.;
#X text 250 389 • Set operations — combining sets with [set.union]
\, [set.intersection] or [set.difference] to build complex queries
and filters.;
#X text 250 444 • Membership tests — quickly checking whether a symbol
or a number has already been seen \, without scanning an entire list.;
#X text 250 499 • Simple tags and labels — storing a small collection
of active tags (for example \, "reverb" \, "delay" \, "chorus") and
enabling or disabling each of them by adding or removing its name.;
#X text 250 569 • Configuration flags — storing a set of enabled options
\, so that the patch can quickly check which options are currently
active.;
